<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Stamp Act 1765</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1765">1765</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Stamp Act of 1765 was a British law requiring printed materials in the American colonies, such as legal documents, newspapers and playing cards, to carry a tax stamp. It was the first direct tax imposed by Parliament on the colonies and provoked riots, boycotts and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stamp-act-congress">Stamp Act Congress</a>, under the slogan of no taxation without representation. Parliament repealed it in 1766 but passed the Declaratory Act asserting its authority.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seven-years-war">Seven Years' War</a> (1756–1763) was won at great expense: the British national debt nearly doubled between 1755 and 1764. The Bute ministry decided in early 1763 to keep ten thousand regulars in the American colonies, at about £225,000 a year. One reason was that disbanding the army would put 1,500 well-connected officers out of work, and Britons disliked a standing army at home. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pontiac">Pontiac</a>'s War, which began in May 1763, led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-proclamation-of-1763">Royal Proclamation of 1763</a> and gave the troops the added duty of keeping peace between Native Americans and colonists.</p><p>Stamp duties had been a successful tax in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a>, raising over £100,000 at little cost, because a document without its stamp was void. When the Sugar Act 1764 passed in April, George Grenville made clear that further taxes, including a stamp tax, might follow. Colonial agents, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benjamin-franklin">Benjamin Franklin</a>, did not expect the intensity of the protest.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>Parliament passed the act on 22 March 1765, to take effect on 1 November 1765. It passed the House of Commons by 205 to 49 and the House of Lords unanimously. Stamped paper from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> had to be used for legal documents, newspapers, pamphlets, playing cards and many other papers, and the tax had to be paid in British currency, which was scarce, rather than colonial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paper-money">paper money</a>. Attorney licences bore the highest tax, £10, cards were taxed a shilling a pack and dice ten shillings. The revenue was to be spent in America, especially on the troops stationed there.</p><p>It was the first internal tax, one based on activities within the colonies, laid directly on them by Parliament. Colonists judged it a graver threat to their rights than the Sugar Act because it reached so much of the colonial economy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Most colonists held that they could be taxed only with the consent of their own assemblies. A member of Parliament argued that colonists were, like the majority of Britons without a vote, represented virtually, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maryland">Maryland</a> lawyer Daniel Dulany rejected the claim in a widely read pamphlet. The Sons of Liberty organised protests, sometimes hanging effigies, and the stamp distributors were intimidated into resigning, so that the tax was never effectively collected. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stamp-act-congress">Stamp Act Congress</a> met in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> in October 1765, with twenty-seven delegates from nine colonies, and petitioned the King and Parliament.</p><p>Grenville was replaced by Lord Rockingham as prime minister on 10 July 1765. British merchants, hurt by colonial non-importation agreements, lobbied for repeal, which Parliament passed on 18 March 1766. It also passed the Declaratory Act, affirming its power to legislate for the colonies in all cases whatsoever. The Sons of Liberty, the committees of correspondence and the congress foreshadowed the opposition to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/townshend-acts">Townshend Acts</a> and the later Continental Congresses.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Stamp Act 1765” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After Britain's victory in the Seven Years' War, Parliament sought to tax the colonies to help pay for their defence, through the <b>Stamp Act</b> of 1765 and the Townshend duties of 1767, provoking protests against taxation without representation.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-states">United States · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Resistance to British taxes grew through the <b>Stamp Act</b> riots, the Boston Massacre of 1770 and the Boston Tea Party of 1773.</blockquote><a href="/state/province-of-massachusetts-bay">Province of Massachusetts Bay</a></li></ul></section>
